; The C target's wiring -- the emit-x64.chiral analogue (E166, SPEC step 4).
;
; emit-x64.chiral is fifteen lines because selecting a target is already a
; ten-line job: hand `emit-with-peep` a Mach and an Alloc and the whole of
; `emit-core` retargets. This module is the same job for `mach-c`, with one
; substitution it cannot make through the existing entry points:
; `emit-with*` all end in `assemble`, which resolves labels into byte
; displacements. On this target the C compiler owns layout and a call is a
; NAME, so the stream must end in `c-assemble` instead. There is no seam for
; that inside `emit-core` and E166's gate G0 forbids cutting one, so the one
; body of `emit-with-peep` is inlined here with `c-assemble` substituted.
; That is the entire difference; the operand -- `emit-program` / `emit-data` /
; `mach-fin` over a conforming Mach -- is byte-for-byte the x64 expression.
;
; No peephole. `x64-peep` is a byte-level register-residency pass with no C
; analogue, and this leg is a verification instrument, not a fast compiler
; (SPEC non-goals). `rd-truthful` (the identity) would be the honest spelling
; if a peephole parameter existed here; there is none, so there is nothing to
; pass.
;
; ── alloc-fixed, and why the choice is a module import ──────────────────────
; `alloc-fixed` (SPEC decision 3) is named at the ONE call site below. The
; earlier draft wanted the type to demand it -- `(Alloc mem-typed)` -- and that
; was measured and rejected (SPEC decision 11b): an index does not lower, and
; it would restate in a type what the module graph already makes
; unrepresentable. `alloc-growing` is not in this blob at all, so there is no
; growing allocator here to wire by mistake.
;
; ── mc-preamble goes FIRST ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
; The shim prototypes cannot ride `fin`: `fin` is a Mach's once-per-program
; TRAILING material by definition (emit-core.chiral:559-561 puts it after the
; literal pool), and C wants a declaration before every use. So `mc-preamble`
; is consed onto the FRONT of the Asm stream. It is plain `a-bytes` text, so
; `c-assemble` passes it through with no function open -- it lands at the head
; of the body, after the forward-declaration prologue `c-assemble` synthesizes.
; That ordering is fine: the prologue declares only emitted functions and
; literal arrays, and depends on nothing the preamble says.
;
; ── the function-name list c-assemble needs ─────────────────────────────────
; `c-assemble` classifies each label ONCE against the emitted function names
; (docs/pattern-boundary-sums.md). Those names are exactly the `name` field of
; each `TIFn` (tal-ir.chiral:49) -- the same value `emit-fn` turns into the
; `(a-label name)` that opens every function (emit-core.chiral:531). So the list
; is a projection of the input, not a second source of truth, and it is
; computed from `fns` here rather than recovered from the stream.

(import "emit-core")
(import "mach-c")
(import "alloc")
(import "alloc-fixed")
(import "c-assemble")

(def ec-fn-name (-> TIFn Str)
  (lam (f) (case f ((ti-fn nm np nr code) nm))))

(declare ec-fn-names (-> (List TIFn) (List Str)))
(def ec-fn-names
  (lam (fs)
    (case fs
      (nil (the (List Str) nil))
      ((cons f rest) (cons (ec-fn-name f) (ec-fn-names rest))))))

; the whole codegen for the C target: one translation unit as Bytes.
(def emit-c (=> (List TIFn) (List Bytes) Bytes)
  (lam (fns lits)
    (c-assemble (ec-fn-names fns)
      (cat2 mc-preamble
        (cat2 (emit-program mc alloc-fixed fns 0 false)
              (cat2 (emit-data mc lits 0) (mach-fin mc)))))))
